The following table reflects the independent Fibonacci estimates recorded prior to team discussion.   Story IDStory NameAli EstMember 2 EstMember 3 EstTeam Agreed Est1Student login with university email32222Browse and search event catalogue23223View detailed event page11214Register for an event58885Cancel an existing registration33536Receive email reminders for events58557Receive push notifications813888Admin can add or edit events35339Admin can remove events223210Handle session tokens securely5585Task 2: AI Comparison & Assumption Audit
The following compares the team's agreed estimates against the AI's estimates, auditing the AI's provided reasoning.   Story NameTeam EstAI EstDifferenceAssumption AuditStudent login with university email231Assumption: AI assumed OAuth but we use manual DB lookup.Browse and search event catalogue220Fact: Relied strictly on the brief.View detailed event page121Assumption: AI assumed rich media handling.Register for an event853Fact: Noted concurrency issues mentioned in text.Cancel an existing registration330Fact: Straightforward DB flag update.Receive email reminders for events532Assumption: AI assumed we have a ready-to-use mail server.Receive push notifications813-5Fact: Noted the complexity of mobile device tokens.Admin can add or edit events330Fact: Standard CRUD operation.Admin can remove events220Fact: Standard CRUD operation.Handle session tokens securely58-3Assumption: AI assumed enterprise-grade JWT requirements.Task 3: Explanation of Estimation GapsReceive push notifications (5-point gap): Our team estimated 8 points, while the AI estimated 13. The AI allowed for a highly complex, enterprise-scale notification service. However, our team knows that our Flutter app is already utilizing Firestore, meaning we can build directly on existing Firebase integration for notification tokens. While spotty campus WiFi makes syncing token state important, the scope for our specific campus event app does not require the massive infrastructure the AI assumed.   Register for an event (3-point gap): Our team estimated 8 points, but the AI underestimated it at 5. The AI assumed a basic database save, but our team knows the local constraints: our Laravel 12 backend must strictly validate COMSATS student IDs (like SP24-BSE). Furthermore, we have to build dual SQLite and Firestore data synchronization so that registrations queue locally in the Flutter app during campus WiFi drops and reconcile when connectivity returns.   Handle session tokens securely (3-point gap): Our team estimated 5 points, while the AI overestimated at 8. The AI assumed enterprise-grade JWT token-rotation policies and SSO. We know our actual scope is a localized campus app built on Laravel 12 and Flutter, which means we only need to implement narrower, standard authentication rather than the complex enterprise setup the AI envisioned.   Task 4: Sprint Capacity & Measurement
Assuming a standard two-week sprint, we are establishing a starting team velocity of 20 points. Based on our team's agreed-upon estimates, we have selected the following high-priority stories that exactly meet our capacity constraint:   Student login with university email (2 points)Browse and search event catalogue (2 points)Admin can add or edit events (3 points)Receive email reminders for events (5 points)Register for an event (8 points)Total Planned: 2 + 2 + 3 + 5 + 8 = 20 points.Because this is a brand new team working in a new codebase, we expect our initial 20-point capacity guess to be an overestimation, and we will measure the actual completed story points at the end of the two-week sprint to find out our true velocity and adjust accordingly. 